d Spain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/112294"} "Mireia Belmonte"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Hosszú may have been sandbagging or simply training through competitions, as she returned to form in Rio, winning this event by almost five seconds over DiRado, in the world record time of 4:26.36, as Belmonte took bronze. Hosszú came to Rio having won 56 international medals, but never an Olympic medal. She would win three golds and four medals in Rio, also winning the 200 IM and the 100 backstroke. DiRado took bronze in the 200 IM but defeated Hosszú in the 200 backstroke to win that gold medal, adding another gold in the 4x200 free relay. Belmonte also added another individual gold, winning the 200 butterfly."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/103469"} "Katinka Hosszú"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} "4:28.58 (1 h5)"] [:td {} "4:26.36 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "World Record"} "WR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/132159"} "Maya DiRado"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "4:33.50 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "4:31.15 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/112294"} "Mireia Belmonte"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ESP"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ESP.png